it is great to be joined today by drew  Penrose who heads the law and policy  department at fair vote he contributes  to work around ranked choice voting  primary elections election  administration and the Voting Rights Act  drew great to talk to you today great to  be on so let's start talking a little  bit about ranked choice voting this is  something that's a significant interest  to my audience first for people who's  who have not yet been introduced to this  concept talk a little bit about  generally how ranked choice voting works  and then we can dig into some of the  specific versions of it absolutely so  rank choice voting is simple you are  allowed to vote for your first choice  just like you do now but in addition to  that you can rank back up choices in  order of preference so if I can't get my  first choice here's my second choice  here's my third choice and so on as many  as you want to rank until you have no  opinion anymore if a candidates able to  get a majority just of those first  choices they'll win just like any other  election but if no candidate has more  than half of the votes in that first  round you eliminate the candidate in the  last place and if your favorite  candidate is eliminated then your vote  instantly counts for your second choice  your backup candidate and you just  repeat that process until a candidate is  able to win with a majority of the vote  so that way more people are able to  participate there's no talk about  spoilers or split votes or plurality  outcomes you get strong winners more  positive campaigns really a pretty big  impact for what is at its heart are  pretty simple small change yeah so let's  talk a little bit about the reasons that  so the advantages to such a system you  talked about the the spoiler effect  would be eliminated because people can  say well you know if I align more with  someone who's outside of the democratic  or Republican Party they might be my  primary choice but I don't have to worry  that I'm going to be effectively helping  the candidate I most disagree with to  win by virtue of supporting this this  other candidate what are some of the  other advantages to such a system so it  allows more candidates to participate  not only so you you emphasize there when  you talked about the spoiler issue the  ability of voters to vote honestly and  that is for  voters the probably the clearest benefit  that you have if you have independent  candidates and candidates from the two  major parties you don't need to think  well do these independents really have a  chance you can just honestly rank rank  the candidates an order of choice  there's a corollary benefit which is  from sort of the candidate or public  side that more candidates are able to  participate without being called  spoilers so if you have a positive  vision for your community and you want  to run for office the thing you don't  need to worry about anymore is whether  you're gonna take votes away from  somebody that you'd like to see when if  you can't that just goes out the window  so as a result you have more inclusive  campaigns already by virtue of having  more candidates able to run so now when  we go yeah I was just gonna say when  when they're out campaigning that  changes things as well because when  you're campaigning you not only want to  earn as many first choices as you can  but you want to make sure that if you  run into a voter who favors one of your  opponents that they're gonna rank you  second so you don't ignore anybody your  incentives are to reach out positively  to as many voters as you can it really  helps to ensure that the ultimate winner  is likely to be somebody that has talked  to a lot more voters than they would  under the current system let's talk  about some of the criticisms that exist  of at least some iterations of what  we're talking about one is the the  possibility that the eventual winner may  not actually have been anybody's first  choice and that that is inherently  problematic some would argue because you  altima tough somebody you have somebody  who has been elected despite not having  been the the sort of ideal candidate for  any of the voters and that that brings  with it problems which versions are  susceptible to that how can you tweak  the system to prevent that from being a  possibility yeah so the system that we  just call ranked choice voting the one  that I described earlier that's not a  mathematical possibility you look at the  first choices initially and if nobody  has a majority of the first choices then  you're going to eliminate the candidates  with the fewest first choices so if a  candidate is  able to attract that kind of core  support they'll be eliminated early in  the count so there's no way that a  candidate can win without achieving that  substantial level of first choices and  and historically we have seen there's  now been you know hundreds of  rank-choice voting elections at the  local level across the California Bay  Area and here in Maryland and other  cities in Minnesota and what we see is  that the candidate who leads in first  choices usually goes on to win I mean if  they're able to attract majority support  they're probably doing pretty well in  first choices as well  sometimes the candidate and second goes  on to in second in first choices goes on  to win after some sort of vote split is  resolved and in exactly one election out  of the hundreds that have taken place a  candidate and third has won that's it no  candidate in fourth or later has ever  gone on to win an election under that  method the methods that do make that  possible are more esoteric sort of more  theoretical systems so for example the  class of voting methods that are called  condor same methods where you actually  compare who you know who is this  candidate beating who are they ranked  higher than and if there's a candidate  that is ranked higher than every other  candidate under every possible iteration  then that can that it always wins  it's used in some university elections  but it's not used in any public  elections anywhere in the world that  system does make it possible for a  candidate to win by essentially being  every voter second choice probably one  of the reasons that it's been difficult  to get it adopted but the Instant Runoff  form that I described does not have that  problem at all okay good work out we're  going through these one by one and I I'm  liking this so here another common  argument is that in some way there's a  violation of one-person one-vote when  you have a ranked choice system this is  a very common one that I hear when I  talk about it and the idea being you're  sort of voting for more than one person  in this ranked way but my my  counter-argument has been in the end in  the final tally  you're only getting one vote is that the  right way to address that criticism  that's exactly right  other ways of putting it but it sounds  like you you understand pretty clearly  what's going on here so you always only  have one vote each round of counting  your vote counts for exactly one  candidate it's never the case that your  vote is helping both your first choice  and your second choice at the same time  your vote only ever accounts for one  candidate at a time  you don't get any benefit from your  candidate being eliminated and at going  to your second choice nobody wants their  favorite candidate to be eliminated and  it's not as if you're getting two votes  whereas other people only got one vote  people generally would prefer that their  vote count for their first choice in  every round of counting and that their  first choice not be eliminated this has  been assessed by courts and courts have  come out the right way every single time  so there have been lawsuits in state  courts in Massachusetts and in Minnesota  that both came out oh and Michigan all  agreeing with that principle and the 9th  Circuit Court of Appeals coming out of a  case in San Francisco also reached the  same conclusion  following his apparent loss under ranked  choice voting Bruce Poliquin the  incumbent and congressional district 2  and Maine filed a lawsuit making this  argument and although it hasn't been  judged on the merits yet an early  decision of the court indicates that the  judge is going to come out the same way  I'm really not worried about it at the  presidential level at the federal level  could any version of these rank-choice  voting systems be put in place without  an amendment to the Constitution so you  couldn't do a national level ranked  choice voting election for president  without either an amendment to the  Constitution or some kind of agreement  among all of the states to share  rank-choice voting data those are the  only two possible ways that that could  occur States could do it for their own  elections so it is already the case that  people have more than two candidates in  the presidential election in their own  states and States don't really like the  idea of all of their electors going to a  candidate who only one you know 47 or 48  percent of the vote when there were  libertarians or greens or other  part of your independent candidates in  the race so state could do it for their  own electors right now it seems that if  you had the successful implementation of  the national the interstate popular vote  compact nationally where you get enough  states to say we will award our electors  to the candidate winning the popular  vote nationally and you could  potentially integrate into that at the  state level a rank-choice voting system  that you could actually drastically  overhaul the way that we select the  president without needing to do a thing  at the federal level I mean it's an  uphill battle but am i correctly  understanding that no that's true  what you would want to do so the  national popular vote compact is an  agreement among states to use the  national popular vote to decide who to  award their electors to yes so they need  a report of the votes from every single  state in order to do that so in order  for that to be a rank choice voting  election you would need essentially  every state to be whole conducting  rank-choice voting so that they would  have the same sort of vote total to  report however the two are not  incompatible so that is to say you could  have some states using rank choice  voting and still have the national  popular vote go into effect you would  just the states that are using ranked  choice voting would report the final  round for the for the national vote  total essentially now you could combine  that with an agreement among the states  to use rank choice voting and to pool  their rank choice voting counts together  and you could get closer and closer to a  national rank choice voting election it  would start to approximate one until you  eventually got to the point where all 50  states had adopted it and then you would  have that without just like you said  without a single action needing to be  taken by the Congress briefly drew I  wanted to touch on one other thing which  is not related specifically to rank  choice but you know we see with every  election that the focus on voter  suppression and intimidation becomes  greater and greater as we get closer to  Election Day  at which point it's often too late to  really stop some of the things that are  going on so given that we just had the  midterms very very recently what can  people be doing right now already to try  to prevent these tactics  succeeding for the 2020 election which I  know sounds like it's so far away but  you we have to start thinking about this  now what can individual voters do there  are a lot of good groups out there that  are running legislative campaigns or  initiative campaigns or filing lawsuits  to try to stop those efforts from taking  place and it is really bipartisan I mean  at the elite level it looks like there  are efforts from partisans to try to  game the system but when you see it go  to the voters for example in Florida the  measure to extend voting rights to more  people after they had completed their  prison sentences passed by like  two-to-one margin in a state that's  pretty closely divided in partisanship  so it's really a nonpartisan idea and  people should be out there promoting  that in addition you know the the  national sort of sentiment being in  favor of a greater right to vote goes  hand-in-hand with our notion that the  right to vote should absolutely be more  than just putting a piece of paper into  a box it should convey real political  power and that's really what reforms  like rank-choice voting are about so I  don't see them really as separate  categories of work at all day they  absolutely are very closely connected  drew Penrose heads the law and policy  Department at fair vote drew really  great talking to you today I appreciate  your time glad to be on thank you very  much

